WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.651
Welcome to Circuit Studio Mastery.

2
00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:08.433
In this tutorial. we will build a voltage divider. edit a component. check the result. and

3
00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:09.471
save the project.

4
00:00:09.721 --> 00:00:15.008
The Parts panel is on the left. the circuit canvas is in the center. and the Study panel is

5
00:00:15.008 --> 00:00:15.843
on the right.

6
00:00:16.093 --> 00:00:18.645
Open or close these panels to make room.

7
00:00:20.445 --> 00:00:24.129
Choose a component from Parts. then click the canvas to place it.

8
00:00:24.379 --> 00:00:26.686
You can also drag a part onto the sheet.

9
00:00:26.936 --> 00:00:31.095
Use a voltage source. two resistors. and ground for this example.

10
00:00:31.345 --> 00:00:36.427
Press Escape when you finish placing a type. so the next click selects rather than adds

11
00:00:36.427 --> 00:00:37.062
another component.

12
00:00:38.862 --> 00:00:42.860
Arrange the source to the left and the resistors in a vertical chain.

13
00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:47.962
Select a resistor and use Rotate. or press R. to turn it ninety degrees.

14
00:00:48.212 --> 00:00:50.646
Keep space between symbols and labels.

15
00:00:50.896 --> 00:00:56.255
The grid helps align terminals. and Fit. or the F key. brings the complete drawing into

16
00:00:56.255 --> 00:00:56.590
view.

17
00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.558
Select Wire. or press W.

18
00:01:00.808 --> 00:01:06.256
Click a component pin to start. click empty space to add a bend. and click the destination

19
00:01:06.256 --> 00:01:07.218
pin to finish.

20
00:01:07.468 --> 00:01:11.210
Connect the source through both resistors and back to the return.

21
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.169
Add ground to that return.

22
00:01:13.419 --> 00:01:18.783
A connected junction has a dot; a plain crossing is not automatically a connection.

23
00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:22.603
Here is the completed divider.

24
00:01:22.853 --> 00:01:25.977
The same branch current flows through both resistors.

25
00:01:26.227 --> 00:01:29.630
The midpoint is the output. measured relative to ground.

26
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.691
With a ten volt source and two equal ten kilo-ohm resistors. predict five volts at the

27
00:01:35.691 --> 00:01:38.597
midpoint before asking the software to calculate it.

28
00:01:42.397 --> 00:01:44.598
Click the lower resistor. R two.

29
00:01:44.848 --> 00:01:49.552
Its reference. value. position. and orientation appear in the Edit panel.

30
00:01:49.802 --> 00:01:53.311
Change the value to twenty kilo-ohms and commit the edit.

31
00:01:53.561 --> 00:01:56.892
You can also double-click a name or value on the drawing.

32
00:01:57.142 --> 00:02:01.398
Before solving. predict whether the midpoint voltage will rise or fall.

33
00:02:03.198 --> 00:02:04.631
Select Show values.

34
00:02:04.881 --> 00:02:11.117
The midpoint is now about six point six seven volts. and the series current is about three

35
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:12.218
hundred thirty-three microamps.

36
00:02:12.468 --> 00:02:16.552
The lower resistor receives a larger share of the source voltage.

37
00:02:16.802 --> 00:02:19.364
These are DC operating-point results.

38
00:02:19.614 --> 00:02:23.762
Use Simulate when you need time waveforms or a frequency response.

39
00:02:25.562 --> 00:02:31.220
Open File and choose Save As to keep the circuit. breadboard. notes. and progress together.

40
00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:35.012
Save circuit only keeps a smaller circuit-focused copy.

41
00:02:35.262 --> 00:02:37.621
Load circuit restores a saved file.

42
00:02:37.871 --> 00:02:42.168
Folder selection depends on your browser; a download is the fallback.

43
00:02:42.418 --> 00:02:49.176
Export gives presentation formats such as SVG or PNG. which do not replace the editable

44
00:02:49.176 --> 00:02:49.627
project.

45
00:02:49.877 --> 00:02:53.600
A circuit project file is different from your course progress file.

46
00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:57.776
Use the floating Save progress button for your complete learning record.
